nation \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c8a81a9-aee0-56a4-b68f-2a2e79bd4516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466017} {:answer/id #uuid "345d3f7c-1e64-52cb-b627-5780060ae9c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "345d3f7c-1e64-52cb-b627-5780060ae9c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466018} :answer-field/id #uuid "a12e1ec7-d047-568a-af4b-b20de2971566" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466852} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d802e20-8c04-5766-bd86-0f77748b6c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466019} {:answer/id #uuid "987c28c3-8e1a-579e-8d11-c523886be91c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "987c28c3-8e1a-579e-8d11-c523886be91c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466020} :answer-field/id #uuid "805d2c07-a9de-52b8-a22a-e1e4e8b45e36" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466853} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "987acaa7-0bbf-55e8-a5ab-af5abd891933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466021} {:answer/id #uuid "fb503b45-96ed-575b-b767-59b553927311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb503b45-96ed-575b-b767-59b553927311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466022} :answer-field/id #uuid "5754b775-f9a5-55de-9c6f-c8ca3b1a0f41" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7119481-1ac5-5669-b8e8-7afd953dbdf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-330309" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n- **I.** $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- **II.** $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- **III.** ${x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)={x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{{x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cef0993c-4542-50d1-85b3-7e4ab1c88cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466023} {:answer/id #uuid "afe220bd-5522-5811-a67a-dd77deb23c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cef0993c-4542-50d1-85b3-7e4ab1c88cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466023} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa33c9fe-228f-5e1f-ba67-6f191bf5bf77" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466855} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64f2895b-3d99-53fe-a08b-3fb528d780b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466025} {:answer/id #uuid "82b170ad-b35c-5c1f-adcd-2ae534c81b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64f2895b-3d99-53fe-a08b-3fb528d780b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466025} :answer-field/id #uuid "93d23d8a-6928-50f0-9072-d270ddd0d007" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466856} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "655cc7d8-fd02-534e-8b8b-651b45c19606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466027} {:answer/id #uuid "bfeccafa-890c-5f83-87b9-43c32dc005d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfeccafa-890c-5f83-87b9-43c32dc005d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466028} :answer-field/id #uuid "053181da-3026-5a83-a668-828f59259c0c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "891dbe2f-f88c-5e79-ae9d-64f7413a702a" :question/math-academy-id "q-330312" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n- **I.** $x(t)={t}^{2}\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$\n- **II.** $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- **III.** $x(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=5,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f3e49a1-1bfa-5e04-8c17-c8857487da1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466029} {:answer/id #uuid "d3353e28-5730-524e-b188-cbc30f9ff7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f3e49a1-1bfa-5e04-8c17-c8857487da1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466029} :answer-field/id #uuid "32fa50d0-6071-515e-b834-6a08e8fef727" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466858} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5f51ac8-4df8-5318-b8ad-738279bb82b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466031} {:answer/id #uuid "cb88a9fa-3d2b-5935-bf50-83bed476c581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb88a9fa-3d2b-5935-bf50-83bed476c581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466032} :answer-field/id #uuid "a14d2d04-e31e-5843-867c-0f561fda2bd9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466859} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc25604f-6d66-554f-9078-a2c52836caf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466033} {:answer/id #uuid "4986099b-8e3a-58d8-b520-7af7c2ec2491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc25604f-6d66-554f-9078-a2c52836caf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466033} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e32f981-d258-539e-a80c-8c4918888146" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fac4a66c-708b-59cc-844a-c54884651cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-330332" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n- **I.** $x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$\n- **II.** $x(t)=-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- **III.** $x(t)={e}^{2t}\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option II is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=0$ and ${c}_{2}=-4,$ respectively.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3f7ae5e-83a5-5eee-b72b-dd7efc63c0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466035} {:answer/id #uuid "517876fa-dd59-539d-a674-2c1f29507c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "517876fa-dd59-539d-a674-2c1f29507c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466036} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef3ca6ba-4998-5c33-8022-e9c4136555bb" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466861} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "878ce675-1899-51cd-a7d3-a9921b8ccea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466037} {:answer/id #uuid "1f12b393-c48e-5a94-bc60-684111e47dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "878ce675-1899-51cd-a7d3-a9921b8ccea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466037} :answer-field/id #uuid "43807068-414e-57ce-9e12-5b33638cbb01" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466862} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aa9028e-ae3b-5d19-8c27-9fe05f3db2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466039} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf982e7-287d-57cb-b33a-12647e5cbc46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9aa9028e-ae3b-5d19-8c27-9fe05f3db2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466039} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bdfa6da-adb2-51cc-a641-4e5b855165cf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89894e08-2da2-5128-a91b-b1054d28b3f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-330353" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n- **I.** $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- **II.** $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- **III.** $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166970 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9740440-bdfb-588e-8f23-2eec9427e449" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Runge-Kutta Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfa7cb1a-07d7-5dc1-8522-44e203a20733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466041} {:answer/id #uuid "9777dad2-5fb7-5723-acbd-2c0bc4c3936a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9777dad2-5fb7-5723-acbd-2c0bc4c3936a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466042} :answer-field/id #uuid "419a60db-e566-534a-950d-ae6b92923121" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466864} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b53c0643-3a96-5862-b0b3-ff8d4fdc399b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466043} {:answer/id #uuid "852e28cd-c93d-5074-b2ad-ae17a24423ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "852e28cd-c93d-5074-b2ad-ae17a24423ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466044} :answer-field/id #uuid "742f823c-da15-5177-97f2-f825acd977b5" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466865} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff1b42cd-6740-5af8-9a96-f5de1d4f6464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466045} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8b6658-8528-5b51-afe2-a95977740b17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8b6658-8528-5b51-afe2-a95977740b17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466046} :answer-field/id #uuid "7002e7f3-60ce-528e-8c65-e48e488b206f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9b7930e-1624-5550-b77a-3086492ab273" :question/math-academy-id "q-331060" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y).$ The table below shows the intermediate quantities computed in the first iteration of the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $3$ |  |\n\nGiven that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ at which of the following points is the function $f$ evaluated during this iteration?\n- **I.** $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2})$\n- **II.** $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{3}{2})$\n- **III.** $(\\frac{3}{2},2)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow, we have the following table of values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $3$ |  |\n\nWith this in mind, let's find each point at which the slope is evaluated in turn using the values given in the table.\n\n- The slope ${y}^{′}$ is evaluated at the initial point $(x,y)=({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(1,2).$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{1}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{2}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{3}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2},\\,2+0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2},2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the slope is evaluated at the points \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc2f3c2c-fd0f-5a5f-880a-82f786d2fea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466047} {:answer/id #uuid "5eafe294-cd87-5ce6-80c3-61c5a84dab38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc2f3c2c-fd0f-5a5f-880a-82f786d2fea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466047} :answer-field/id #uuid "28a05091-6793-5cc0-b4eb-1ce640b010f7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466867} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d27ea8d-6973-5d55-8a0b-9bf18916d7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466049} {:answer/id #uuid "150d1418-d007-5c9d-a8f6-7a8d44e86905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "150d1418-d007-5c9d-a8f6-7a8d44e86905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466050} :answer-field/id #uuid "55f68aea-74bc-534e-824e-8c810de35f10" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466868} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "364859a7-aaff-5174-bdd3-5f50d200ce9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466051} {:answer/id #uuid "ce486bda-79e9-5dcc-b216-bbf4e590960e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "364859a7-aaff-5174-bdd3-5f50d200ce9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466051} :answer-field/id #uuid "4abec42a-e2c5-5ff8-8ec3-56a8bfa4ed73" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "363ad515-bb3d-517b-aff5-449014ea0698" :question/math-academy-id "q-331114" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n- **I.** ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- **II.** ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-8{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- **III.** ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-8{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fde37f96-d46e-5cee-9ab6-aab431ae55be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466053} {:answer/id #uuid "2699930e-d019-57fc-8022-2330d11ba5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fde37f96-d46e-5cee-9ab6-aab431ae55be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466053} :answer-field/id #uuid "9199a8be-d8cd-521d-aac3-8b79249a4c7e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466870} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9767513f-6a1c-5067-8d17-1a73876affa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466055} {:answer/id #uuid "db919368-5b43-5056-b22a-11edeb465f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9767513f-6a1c-5067-8d17-1a73876affa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466055} :answer-field/id #uuid "69419699-98d7-52af-997a-ad1c53e03a9b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466871} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22581b05-f6ca-56cf-9273-21259bfec6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466057} {:answer/id #uuid "3227479f-2c9a-502d-bda7-ec628fe6ba9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3227479f-2c9a-502d-bda7-ec628fe6ba9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466058} :answer-field/id #uuid "3be0b716-41e6-5218-9989-d96369208af2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "164f3277-5bbd-5470-9288-18ef536cb19b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331974" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n- **I.** ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- **II.** ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- **III.** ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d810721-64de-536e-8ec7-44fde4184215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466059} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6fec79-351c-59dc-8c17-b118e54669f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb6fec79-351c-59dc-8c17-b118e54669f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466060} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b061f2-eb0c-52ec-995e-e755959f6065" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466873} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "384b8250-df38-58c3-bf55-21334be56f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466061} {:answer/id #uuid "02da0336-291f-5f57-90d0-d178e7f896ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "384b8250-df38-58c3-bf55-21334be56f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466061} :answer-field/id #uuid "c41e7cca-531f-5697-b5a4-601c968d6d43" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466874} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74bf66fe-9855-5a8d-891f-9c88a9651c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466063} {:answer/id #uuid "2282a1cd-d3ab-590a-9a34-f31c344ca632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74bf66fe-9855-5a8d-891f-9c88a9651c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466063} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e3c2c46-97b3-559d-97ab-d711e3149ccf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfb33e06-625f-5894-a78d-d1f418d2b822" :question/math-academy-id "q-331976" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n- **I.** ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- **II.** ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- **III.** ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e50e8ab-e411-586f-a3d4-dc2e4fcd7cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466065} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8afd2e-ae6c-570d-a8ff-76907c67d059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc8afd2e-ae6c-570d-a8ff-76907c67d059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466066} :answer-field/id #uuid "62b9e8dd-90d0-51c7-be44-1b2eb0a32755" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c56cefe7-8454-534e-b476-cc7a1d062420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466067} {:answer/id #uuid "0cfa897d-e2a7-5a0a-9895-d4e337ada051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c56cefe7-8454-534e-b476-cc7a1d062420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466067} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1061fc5-5342-526c-88c1-d457329519a0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466877} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e25c152c-cde8-5b9b-a39d-1c434bef3856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466069} {:answer/id #uuid "2db53487-34f5-58b2-a4f4-a19aaa44c64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e25c152c-cde8-5b9b-a39d-1c434bef3856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466069} :answer-field/id #uuid "c682ebed-3244-5b41-a799-d3bf75036c38" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466878} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3833cd4-b47d-541f-9a24-3a41b77197eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466071} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5fdda5-251a-5d00-8098-412f1be153f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c5fdda5-251a-5d00-8098-412f1be153f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466072} :answer-field/id #uuid "030e73ec-670f-572f-9967-01c8e343d51e" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30787f50-63d0-5577-9588-239251d45d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-333092" :question/problem "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n- **I.** $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n- **II.** $\\lambda Δx=2.0$\n- **III.** $\\lambda Δx=2.4$\n- **IV.** $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.0$ falls *outside* of the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.4$ falls *outside* the given region, and\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ falls *within* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is stable for $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ and $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c054bd0-f5aa-5673-839d-60c70e36ecde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466073} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec5a82a-15e5-55c1-9ec0-77d8f98d7390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ec5a82a-15e5-55c1-9ec0-77d8f98d7390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466074} :answer-field/id #uuid "304a716b-ed43-5878-9709-b47f3885c2e2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466880} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4196f37b-b17e-5500-b365-38933d8c6fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466075} {:answer/id #uuid "ae04d060-cb4c-53f4-abc0-3ea635425742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4196f37b-b17e-5500-b365-38933d8c6fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466075} :answer-field/id #uuid "b921d865-c281-522b-a275-43c0069d9b9c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466881} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "309ea663-119a-555b-9de9-53704383119a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466077} {:answer/id #uuid "280dd247-2fa0-514a-be07-100c657f77b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "309ea663-119a-555b-9de9-53704383119a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466077} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a8d88b1-cd72-5a27-b151-965e6f804fb3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ad56467-9a9c-5515-8f2a-3d6fb0d92b9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333311" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3y+x$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1$\n- **III.** $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+5$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Riccati differential equation with \n\n$$\na(x)={x}^{2}+1,\\,b(x)=-3,\\,c(x)=x.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{5}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{5}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8aa7472-bbd4-5c0a-9f72-9ce28aa1f610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466079} {:answer/id #uuid "24eb1ff0-d74d-5df8-8296-0484e57e9a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8aa7472-bbd4-5c0a-9f72-9ce28aa1f610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466079} :answer-field/id #uuid "85fa4d6e-455a-5cdd-a6be-26c7d529fe1b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466883} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe67d143-10d1-52d5-8bda-e97f745215c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466081} {:answer/id #uuid "22997aaf-13ec-5c18-9723-c27e1c92bb1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe67d143-10d1-52d5-8bda-e97f745215c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466081} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31af6a4-ddb6-5d83-a0be-d42d0844a09d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8130c4e-6214-583b-b0a0-b674a23426a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466083} {:answer/id #uuid "999fc675-5d81-54ab-9955-d557da83a00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "999fc675-5d81-54ab-9955-d557da83a00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466084} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c9f982c-7369-5ecb-a85e-b5422d0616bf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2271166c-28ab-5bb4-b5b8-f402b78156b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333316" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+5$\n- **II.** $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}-4y=(x-2){y}^{2}+{e}^{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+5.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation II is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{4}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{4}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\n- Equation III is a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-2){y}^{2}+{e}^{x}+4y.\n$$\n\nThis is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=x-2,\\,b(x)=4,\\,c(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8b89f26-adad-575c-8f8a-18624e38817d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466085} {:answer/id #uuid "8c088e93-db28-5af4-8cea-02634faa9f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8b89f26-adad-575c-8f8a-18624e38817d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466085} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ee4c8eb-22be-53e4-a3bc-6b780a4d30a7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466886} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52f914e7-a1ae-5e28-a415-434c06a1f1de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466087} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d23d7e-183c-5ff9-8180-a24422676acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0d23d7e-183c-5ff9-8180-a24422676acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466088} :answer-field/id #uuid "49582718-6055-571a-a46c-d4af70065cb3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466887} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf65d349-d615-5d9f-b082-913281fe1aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466089} {:answer/id #uuid "28309f62-d15b-5b18-8620-3195fd791c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf65d349-d615-5d9f-b082-913281fe1aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466089} :answer-field/id #uuid "18eb13b6-180c-5209-be56-839868507b00" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2cc3f6c-b7da-5ffb-9443-884962200fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-333321" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-xy+1$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+3){y}^{2}-4y+{e}^{x}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-xy+1.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation II is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)={x}^{2}+3,\\,b(x)=-4,\\,c(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c392cf1-1167-5adf-a2ad-cb7ba649470e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466091} {:answer/id #uuid "c91d6800-6a34-5e04-9e4d-09fdc446964d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c91d6800-6a34-5e04-9e4d-09fdc446964d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466092} :answer-field/id #uuid "3773776b-c4fa-5eaa-894f-b159e277b227" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466889} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84eedbce-c9a4-5756-850f-9f31a1f96a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466093} {:answer/id #uuid "2170eb96-db98-5f50-a545-b496817ebbc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84eedbce-c9a4-5756-850f-9f31a1f96a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466093} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e444b71-ef3e-5e45-ae6a-dd1ac93fd8a9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466890} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f411f329-9c25-5838-9474-9b33f4fb1845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466095} {:answer/id #uuid "b827daf3-b4aa-52f6-9229-2fd4e815ba9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f411f329-9c25-5838-9474-9b33f4fb1845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466095} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0b54d48-e87f-5904-8ac7-665b250fd231" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f788c2b2-09c8-57a5-9839-705d54ca27d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333322" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}+2xy+{x}^{2}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}-y+{e}^{x}$\n- **III.** $y{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=x-1,\\,b(x)=2x,\\,c(x)={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}-y+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation.Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1)y-\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{3}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cacad9b6-d96e-5ed3-8941-1b425e4561b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466097} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca4bf25-4a12-5fe7-b403-40cd2d1ae306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cacad9b6-d96e-5ed3-8941-1b425e4561b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466097} :answer-field/id #uuid "52118cf3-1913-5b6f-a6a4-3a44d5057427" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466892} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16526fc2-0575-5da4-9643-0dde15f08f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466099} {:answer/id #uuid "1b41930f-abcc-5604-be55-86986ebf53ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16526fc2-0575-5da4-9643-0dde15f08f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466099} :answer-field/id #uuid "516c2af8-6bdf-5d44-a25e-66e659569bb0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466893} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1fdffc-3d4c-5f3c-b3fd-1c4e03051b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466101} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d8859c-a702-5bff-9977-f3bbc69941ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8d8859c-a702-5bff-9977-f3bbc69941ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466102} :answer-field/id #uuid "a659403e-5966-58dd-8b52-749c8e0a467c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81f632f0-dedf-5479-9362-313bdb74c812" :question/math-academy-id "q-333693" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}$\n- **II.** $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2}$\n- **III.** $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $-3(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $(Δx)^{4}=(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Clairaut Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "393bdc2e-0c53-55d1-b42d-f21779c96991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466103} {:answer/id #uuid "cc37c253-ca9d-582a-a042-d4ba0cd193cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "393bdc2e-0c53-55d1-b42d-f21779c96991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466103} :answer-field/id #uuid "63a0ee9f-d140-5766-a139-2ccf0ede2b4a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30fa331e-24c9-5e82-a126-f6b2be1efb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466105} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8a59f0-ab40-5ca8-85e2-b700ce7b070f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30fa331e-24c9-5e82-a126-f6b2be1efb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466105} :answer-field/id #uuid "42bd73d3-67cb-5237-b6b1-d25f0f7228f2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f2a89d1-04d6-5233-9814-a549170ce1cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466107} {:answer/id #uuid "8315e054-5367-5c7b-9e40-75f94b3e5e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8315e054-5367-5c7b-9e40-75f94b3e5e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466108} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed7611aa-0014-5bd2-aa34-2729fec9a6ed" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "077e1927-c6a2-59ff-bf00-a9d36509bb47" :question/math-academy-id "q-334000" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n- **I.** $y={x}^{3}{y}^{′}+x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$\n- **II.** $x{y}^{4}-xy{y}^{′}-{y}^{2}=0$\n- **III.** $y=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. In fact, it cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx{y}^{3}-x{y}^{′}-y=0.\n$$\n\nAgain, this equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b10c1f0-1c23-5e6a-a638-00d4206c3941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466109} {:answer/id #uuid "d28a6002-55cd-5f7f-bbe3-33e7f86078a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d28a6002-55cd-5f7f-bbe3-33e7f86078a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466110} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b58cc72-a7dc-5a38-80f8-5fdd3ef64402" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466898} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f3abbc4-dcb2-5435-b51f-36e87dfc500c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466111} {:answer/id #uuid "931ce38c-be5f-547d-9880-9d2b3317aecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f3abbc4-dcb2-5435-b51f-36e87dfc500c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466111} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2297588-9a94-580d-b537-017ece5f690d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466899} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e908df6-2015-5d31-8b11-a92c49fe763d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466113} {:answer/id #uuid "826c6c52-f84b-564d-a8da-c33eae270dd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e908df6-2015-5d31-8b11-a92c49fe763d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466113} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f7df6f-2935-5529-af4c-9c1d183d5d81" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466900} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "680a020b-b7cf-5cda-ac74-7abef601edd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466115} {:answer/id #uuid "4605055f-d4f1-5f54-b932-4a05194abc44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4605055f-d4f1-5f54-b932-4a05194abc44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466116} :answer-field/id #uuid "4312f87c-513a-51c7-bf55-5d257c752a3d" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3f7955cf-0d1a-5a59-88f5-cf30308c87f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-334008" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **stable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n- **I.** $Δx=\\frac{0.25}{\\lambda }$\n- **II.** $Δx=\\frac{3.5}{\\lambda }$\n- **III.** $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda }$\n- **IV.** $Δx=\\frac{1.5}{\\lambda }$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.25}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.25.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<0.25 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{3.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=3.5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.5.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<1.5 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowl