For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n- **I.** $\\lambda Δx=0.1$\n- **II.** $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n- **III.** $\\lambda Δx=2.9$\n- **IV.** $\\lambda Δx=3.4$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.1)| & =|1-0.1+\\frac{(0.1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.1)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.1)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{72\\,387}{80\\,000}| \\\\  & =0.9048…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|1-1.2+\\frac{(1.2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1.2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1.2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{199}{625}| \\\\  & =0.3184<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.9)| & =|1-2.9+\\frac{(2.9)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.9)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.9)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{284\\,921}{240\\,000}| \\\\  & =1.1871…>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.4)| & =|1-3.4+\\frac{(3.4)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3.4)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3.4)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{11\\,987}{5000}| \\\\  & =2.3974>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5f3dbb6-3236-5c63-aae4-575fdea2d15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466213} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca860ee-7207-549a-b929-a3d3c4e8679a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ca860ee-7207-549a-b929-a3d3c4e8679a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466214} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce5ea6f3-8732-5575-b6a3-b67fb7a1d1ee" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466950} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f29f036-f1ca-52cf-97b3-96d5958e4c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466215} {:answer/id #uuid "c842b6ef-a0f1-5ab5-a956-857848597929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c842b6ef-a0f1-5ab5-a956-857848597929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466216} :answer-field/id #uuid "d21ee857-a11b-5609-a5c6-08baa0ce968d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466951} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cc3a3e4-402c-55ad-ae38-3f8336d22a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466217} {:answer/id #uuid "be9e186e-25fb-5dfc-b4a8-071a4b8a1ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be9e186e-25fb-5dfc-b4a8-071a4b8a1ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466218} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e593dda-1295-5d71-b1c9-dbad427fbfa3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466952} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b4dc7e-6ec2-5408-ae71-ff930cd18cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466219} {:answer/id #uuid "94b4c169-c254-5a0a-b54b-72a7559a691c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94b4c169-c254-5a0a-b54b-72a7559a691c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466220} :answer-field/id #uuid "7983d893-d643-5aea-89b6-39f7b9e973fc" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ca57581-1de5-5ee9-ad10-ddff45b3ad82" :question/math-academy-id "q-334466" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the trapezoidal method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.15$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=0.8$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=2.9$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=6.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the trapezoidal method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1-\\lambda Δx/2}{1+\\lambda Δx/2}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\text{ }\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx)\\,{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the trapezoidal method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1-\\lambda Δx/2}{1+\\lambda Δx/2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the trapezoidal method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.15)| & =|\\frac{1-0.15/2}{1+0.15/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{0.925}{1.075}| \\\\  & =0.8604…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.8)| & =|\\frac{1-0.8/2}{1+0.8/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{0.6}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.4285…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.9)| & =|\\frac{1-2.9/2}{1+2.9/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-0.45}{2.45}| \\\\  & =0.1836…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=6.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(6.2)| & =|\\frac{1-6.2/2}{1+6.2/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-2.1}{4.1}| \\\\  & =0.5121…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “I, II, III, and IV”.\n\nNote that, since the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f64945e6-461b-5d18-9b99-f35f9b355641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466221} {:answer/id #uuid "d38bb07f-751f-54c9-b764-f51322e2668e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d38bb07f-751f-54c9-b764-f51322e2668e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466222} :answer-field/id #uuid "98041717-5f24-5e16-a3a2-90815508b17e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466954} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8ec9503-912b-581e-9101-5ab75ff6539c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466223} {:answer/id #uuid "315983b8-bbe1-5897-a9ed-d36631a2f314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "315983b8-bbe1-5897-a9ed-d36631a2f314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466224} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5682c0e-d95c-5e6a-bf2b-0b52a5ac0462" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466955} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16746afe-e153-51d1-9eb9-34d0d1a6337f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466225} {:answer/id #uuid "cb70e508-bafd-5367-be0b-778bcf31773f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16746afe-e153-51d1-9eb9-34d0d1a6337f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466225} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd335144-cc54-5989-87e1-0c977e9f9451" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466956} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27ffd1a6-4dfe-5d3b-aa58-0855c34f913d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466227} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3779ef-8b05-5f47-8678-14ecfe117874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27ffd1a6-4dfe-5d3b-aa58-0855c34f913d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466227} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ecf58e-ece9-5338-abfe-fd3aa4eb4738" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "faa06cb1-bef4-588f-8333-1536faf16397" :question/math-academy-id "q-334627" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda =4$ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n- **I.** $Δx=0.2$\n- **II.** $Δx=0.45$\n- **III.** $Δx=0.9$\n- **IV.** $Δx=2.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda =4,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <4Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.45$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.9$ doesn't satisfy the inequality, and\n\n- $Δx=2.2$ doesn't satisfy the inequality.\n\nTherefore, Euler’s method is stable for step sizes $Δx=0.2$ and $Δx=0.45.$\n\nThus, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34049d3f-00ce-564e-96be-3d4a415e9d03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466229} {:answer/id #uuid "025fb1cb-179b-5f42-b287-f22266e61c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "025fb1cb-179b-5f42-b287-f22266e61c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466230} :answer-field/id #uuid "b709d5ba-ac4c-5862-acb2-b835e4dbe669" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466958} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4362b8e9-1f7b-546a-a6b8-187913b71183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466231} {:answer/id #uuid "540f0244-5411-52c3-b065-08e5639bd430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "540f0244-5411-52c3-b065-08e5639bd430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466232} :answer-field/id #uuid "69c25310-2454-5447-bf22-ca7004696e62" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466959} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb65072a-b651-59bb-9382-c6146f387679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466233} {:answer/id #uuid "88faea08-1274-5db8-ba34-a39cf7d5c460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb65072a-b651-59bb-9382-c6146f387679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466233} :answer-field/id #uuid "3522f90e-4432-5dcc-9661-d8b266fef945" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466960} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ea56215-b47e-57b7-ac03-0716004b3281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466235} {:answer/id #uuid "d5cb17f7-ed3c-5ef9-951f-f032839b6d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ea56215-b47e-57b7-ac03-0716004b3281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466235} :answer-field/id #uuid "d86196da-55a6-55e7-8cb3-5d5c3464396b" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "331acb28-49e8-50e4-b8d3-d67c401cc67e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334715" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the modified Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n- **I.** $\\lambda Δx=0.5$\n- **II.** $\\lambda Δx=1.6$\n- **III.** $\\lambda Δx=2.2$\n- **IV.** $\\lambda Δx=3.5$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the modified Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.5)| & =|1-0.5+\\frac{(0.5)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{5}{8}| \\\\  & =0.625<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.6)| & =|1-1.6+\\frac{(1.6)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{17}{25}| \\\\  & =0.68<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.2)| & =|1-2.2+\\frac{(2.2)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{61}{50}| \\\\  & =1.22>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.5)| & =|1-3.5+\\frac{(3.5)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{29}{8}| \\\\  & =3.625>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the Modified Euler Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb0d4624-31c1-5af9-b2e0-9885a5ffbf81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466237} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ef7957-ae37-5ee5-885b-be3ded28497f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ef7957-ae37-5ee5-885b-be3ded28497f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466238} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3bf1298-b976-5ed4-9372-2489cb9d1ced" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466962} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08026d37-ae55-5d34-9bcc-d8e548575663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466239} {:answer/id #uuid "8063038f-941e-52f3-93c4-85664a2399ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08026d37-ae55-5d34-9bcc-d8e548575663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466239} :answer-field/id #uuid "c24cfc24-beef-542c-935e-e7f46ce55c58" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466963} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53ee9267-5452-5d1a-9bfd-69bb803508d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466241} {:answer/id #uuid "6f57c2d1-dd26-5064-87d8-7c3c79fcf019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53ee9267-5452-5d1a-9bfd-69bb803508d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466241} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbf0dbcc-9310-5cda-bd40-1c087cca8de1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "512c526c-f958-5c94-ad6a-b60bd207799d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334745" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $11(Δx)^{5}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $5$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.2Δx.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37c13af1-cf3a-5a14-8bae-33a89aac6276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466243} {:answer/id #uuid "436c6954-c10e-53c9-afef-65197a273f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37c13af1-cf3a-5a14-8bae-33a89aac6276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466243} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a0c7074-8edd-5541-8934-3ad84b09bf2b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466965} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40e69603-d1d0-5452-b44b-2966220faf8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466245} {:answer/id #uuid "fff72690-8c78-5971-8928-e0fcc2de06df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40e69603-d1d0-5452-b44b-2966220faf8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466245} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dbad88e-e06a-5e60-84c3-ee8fb0b39767" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466966} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "171b9921-ff47-565b-a344-fe20fa223385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466247} {:answer/id #uuid "224d30ab-0d64-5c02-b4dc-23b7646a4076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "224d30ab-0d64-5c02-b4dc-23b7646a4076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466248} :answer-field/id #uuid "632ce865-5b13-5a54-96d8-5ee543ad576a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5074782-bb53-5a41-89b0-5eb1637aa0c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334752" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{6}+5(Δx)^{7}$\n- **II.** $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{9}$\n- **III.** $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}+3(Δx)^{8}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{6}+5(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{6}=2(Δx)^{5+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $5$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{9},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{4}=0.4(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}+3(Δx)^{8},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{5}=6(Δx)^{4+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aedc923c-72ce-55c1-b263-76f8db67116f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466249} {:answer/id #uuid "9010ce6e-b598-590d-b9a2-18cff964d492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aedc923c-72ce-55c1-b263-76f8db67116f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466249} :answer-field/id #uuid "43d60d1e-98f2-52a4-8d07-5cb5d4365198" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466968} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "471ec00b-093e-5bc7-99f3-26a5c33c4727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466251} {:answer/id #uuid "4e67c4f6-15f7-5a9a-baea-58b380116716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e67c4f6-15f7-5a9a-baea-58b380116716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466252} :answer-field/id #uuid "f79108d7-c739-56ef-a601-e5756333be9f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466969} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c49ee3a4-4e30-5e14-b26b-eb9722906e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466253} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c7ec24-1d8c-5f3a-aa79-d2c2f775c5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c49ee3a4-4e30-5e14-b26b-eb9722906e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466253} :answer-field/id #uuid "711b3c68-a68d-5900-b533-50c6e5c3e48a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdca2386-e70b-5926-863b-7ba03141d57e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334753" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+4(Δx)^{5}$\n- **II.** $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6}$\n- **III.** $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{3}=0.3(Δx)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{5}=9(Δx)^{4+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f3f4c1d-54b2-5ebb-9d13-62d90fb171fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466255} {:answer/id #uuid "8a413ece-9c38-5a1d-894a-c619dbde078a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f3f4c1d-54b2-5ebb-9d13-62d90fb171fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466255} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf43fc41-6e02-552a-b5e1-b0f59ef053ea" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466971} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb16386a-b8ad-5910-9e2d-68e28e1f9962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466257} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3eef4f-85f1-561e-9c2c-9be7a8f7de4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb16386a-b8ad-5910-9e2d-68e28e1f9962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466257} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4473fa4-5e10-5c6f-b5b8-887dc5eb2dce" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466972} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c1ed169-9367-5354-9f26-375b8eee140a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466259} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5b1e05-de4a-553e-a7da-ba2687c5f114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a5b1e05-de4a-553e-a7da-ba2687c5f114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466260} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc97e70a-d0a0-5ec5-8321-34314729924b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3ccb3bf-e44e-5f18-88ca-d013183f6901" :question/math-academy-id "q-334755" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $τ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+6(Δx)^{6}$\n- **II.** $τ(Δx)=0.8(Δx)^{2}+5(Δx)^{3}$\n- **III.** $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+6(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{3}=4(Δx)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.8(Δx)^{2}+5(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.8(Δx)^{2}=0.8(Δx)^{1+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{4}=9(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21ffaded-0ccb-5f13-b811-33701f6c5711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466261} {:answer/id #uuid "0396eff4-577a-5f94-9927-4f8eed41fc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0396eff4-577a-5f94-9927-4f8eed41fc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466262} :answer-field/id #uuid "f14c996d-e452-507e-9e46-0a859b3d4fa9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466974} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dde4f68b-d7ce-5557-91ee-694beaffacba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466263} {:answer/id #uuid "df314c42-1898-5b47-ae5d-cfb5a8bb2afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df314c42-1898-5b47-ae5d-cfb5a8bb2afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466264} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bc8d993-fe81-5ea2-837e-05e1c8f91a4f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466975} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aa778e3-a751-5e3c-bbce-c3d6e285508d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466265} {:answer/id #uuid "a2808125-76aa-5fe5-a9e0-5a2d101ac8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aa778e3-a751-5e3c-bbce-c3d6e285508d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466265} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3599088-0440-5c81-8639-94ce7a9bf76b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1edbdd20-a6fa-5411-be9e-ad5ea2edf0c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334756" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6}$\n- **II.** $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$\n- **III.** $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+6(Δx)^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{3}=4(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a19bd48-ecf7-5542-aef9-b80c339d0e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466267} {:answer/id #uuid "99d0f073-a9aa-50db-a37a-0b5fbd6f58c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99d0f073-a9aa-50db-a37a-0b5fbd6f58c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466268} :answer-field/id #uuid "647b4cf5-2656-5712-a376-9a24401a1932" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466977} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f10ecb-6a81-5ba9-9856-6aaaec40c480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466269} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bbd6dd-71be-52f7-bf35-59d8ee250f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08f10ecb-6a81-5ba9-9856-6aaaec40c480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466269} :answer-field/id #uuid "acdbfa81-5891-564f-b38e-9aca9b11f529" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466978} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80021749-582b-5c45-993a-3e289cf5583b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466271} {:answer/id #uuid "30d77a63-d315-5188-91b1-75b4d236a957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30d77a63-d315-5188-91b1-75b4d236a957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466272} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d1638c3-109d-5aeb-8697-28c36ac866f4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b0cbcd0-f13f-5586-9c22-f5d860877a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-334757" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6}$\n- **II.** $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$\n- **III.** $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{3}=2(Δx)^{3}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{5}=0.4(Δx)^{4+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9074c277-7083-52b7-9d92-f90b79edda0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466273} {:answer/id #uuid "3d43ca0e-6b9a-5360-bf7b-fc4b343a42e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d43ca0e-6b9a-5360-bf7b-fc4b343a42e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466274} :answer-field/id #uuid "067cd356-6a52-5fff-bf1f-661ef57405d9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466980} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "524b4010-feab-5741-a5b6-c80bea1db028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466275} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9c3d6c-3410-51a0-910d-c381144e9445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb9c3d6c-3410-51a0-910d-c381144e9445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466276} :answer-field/id #uuid "20f6961f-1c69-53d9-869f-79a49c25c997" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466981} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35d49689-9636-518d-846f-27322705257a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466277} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9bd4fd-fe8f-573e-907e-4e03e6095642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35d49689-9636-518d-846f-27322705257a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466277} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0e616a8-f854-577b-97f9-bd9a20287da4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a147217d-4ada-5afd-bf77-eb76079d5271" :question/math-academy-id "q-334761" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4}$\n- **II.** $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7}$\n- **III.** $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.5(Δx)^{4}=0.5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfee73e7-0112-5b10-9471-39a9a18f611b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466279} {:answer/id #uuid "f2522a59-62fc-5f5a-b7eb-96387dca5f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2522a59-62fc-5f5a-b7eb-96387dca5f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466280} :answer-field/id #uuid "c38575ec-7d37-5f69-888c-de6fa827bcaa" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b993091-02a1-5ea4-b695-173f1843de57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466281} {:answer/id #uuid "26c00063-63de-57f4-a9e4-1ac6e46eda75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b993091-02a1-5ea4-b695-173f1843de57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466281} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d737a1f-7f59-55e7-985d-ab977e42c4cc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466984} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9846bdec-c728-5080-91e0-da12b46ab5e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466283} {:answer/id #uuid "54ae4d8c-42e7-5689-850a-2615c9fb1102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9846bdec-c728-5080-91e0-da12b46ab5e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466283} :answer-field/id #uuid "96efe50c-03ce-5f7d-8d01-83d2857f6dfc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05b85b1b-94cc-5955-8bdf-0e035f03c881" :question/math-academy-id "q-334851" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- **II.** $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$\n- **III.** $f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0$" :question/worked-solution "For $f(x)=3x^5-2x$, we have $f(x)\\to0$ as $x\\to0$, so $f$ is bounded near zero and is $O(1)$.\n\nFor statement II, $|f(x)/x^2|=|3x^3-2/x|$ is unbounded near zero, so $f$ is not $O(x^2)$. For statement III, $|f(x)/x^3|=|3x^2-2/x^2|\\to\\infty$, so $f$ is not $O(x^3)$.\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, and statements II and III are false. The correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20c75eac-0251-58fc-9409-e3c4fbb0db53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466285} {:answer/id #uuid "679d0c57-b841-573a-a685-4a8622782fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "679d0c57-b841-573a-a685-4a8622782fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466286} :answer-field/id #uuid "68da005c-225d-5113-8d8b-13cea0f5abd0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466986} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83972ef9-8c66-548b-beca-5689ea6208b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466287} {:answer/id #uuid "1c51d4c0-55aa-5641-9e5b-281e3384a135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83972ef9-8c66-548b-beca-5689ea6208b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466287} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc0a24d0-4e27-5bdf-becf-739116d3d53d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466987} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecadbcf1-48ad-521b-9da0-d951f5ab919a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466289} {:answer/id #uuid "1803bd10-5f24-5896-8105-fbd58c0288ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecadbcf1-48ad-521b-9da0-d951f5ab919a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466289} :answer-field/id #uuid "3273f403-4f50-5518-ab19-640d36d78393" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e125563-3523-5218-87f9-08cecc055fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335020" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- **II.** $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- **III.** $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{2}+5+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "854dcc3e-3ad0-587b-951a-1d8efb42c49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466291} {:answer/id #uuid "87fb8413-2de6-5e83-b378-21c6a8148bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "854dcc3e-3ad0-587b-951a-1d8efb42c49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466291} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c90c71-7a48-5daf-b1a5-0da553196670" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466989} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dff35190-c385-5ff4-a38a-7e9adcbec5c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466293} {:answer/id #uuid "24d572c3-ed70-5b8f-a429-60b32f8ef502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24d572c3-ed70-5b8f-a429-60b32f8ef502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466294} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac2b463a-b8e4-5b1b-8ad2-7388bb6d3330" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466990} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ceea4e5-9793-50bb-8c56-b2d422525637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466295} {:answer/id #uuid "97841c80-6e56-5adb-9408-75ee7c8afebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97841c80-6e56-5adb-9408-75ee7c8afebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466296} :answer-field/id #uuid "040257a7-e75b-5b8f-94b5-f87d56de22ed" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a8a1841-dc47-5a84-bd2d-e5c201943d9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335246" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{1}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2tx}{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{1+x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊂(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{1}{(1+x+t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b300e0b9-d29d-5f4a-b4c6-ddf029e8438c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466297} {:answer/id #uuid "048f2c66-c0d9-54d0-b540-10593e6a06b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "048f2c66-c0d9-54d0-b540-10593e6a06b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466298} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb212ca-feab-55fb-bbe5-bf2eaadf951c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466992} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dbd657e-103b-5d31-b5c6-791565f66d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466299} {:answer/id #uuid "569d20a0-8036-5d36-bb7b-12d1ef5dc3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "569d20a0-8036-5d36-bb7b-12d1ef5dc3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466300} :answer-field/id #uuid "eafc1e72-ec71-5219-9230-3bf6c0ee7ba2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466993} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58b98bf5-3fd9-5b73-9571-b262aca2b590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466301} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d11a7e-0e24-5812-8b81-e408a2fa4534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7d11a7e-0e24-5812-8b81-e408a2fa4534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466302} :answer-field/id #uuid "e69c2a12-6035-509f-9da1-9d85906ac437" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9de6d57f-11dc-5bb2-bebc-d38ef2773685" :question/math-academy-id "q-335247" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>1$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\sqrt{x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+t}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$ (since $x+t\\ge c>0$ on $R$). Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2x{t}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t>1$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ never vanishes on $R,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a4f09b1-f161-5622-a23d-b0ffdfb2d435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466303} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd5f489-5a61-5601-8ecc-98a5906dddc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcd5f489-5a61-5601-8ecc-98a5906dddc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466304} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e605653-b47a-5ef7-9a12-6a6e858e1bbf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466995} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22edb3de-f55b-58a1-9692-099dbd00066f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466305} {:answer/id #uuid "581893f8-2232-5da0-be5a-6d7f06e1d7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22edb3de-f55b-58a1-9692-099dbd00066f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466305} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c8b9f4c-0d1d-538d-9987-3817690edcc4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466996} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a13b11-4489-5b2f-bce4-6df115f00f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466307} {:answer/id #uuid "31dd6c34-6e6b-519e-82c4-6849b3fc2a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31dd6c34-6e6b-519e-82c4-6849b3fc2a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466308} :answer-field/id #uuid "29ee4bc8-90a4-5de9-b5bb-ad229b502589" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57d1f543-d099-580f-9280-d60da64177c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-335248" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n- **I.** ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- **II.** ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{|x-t|}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- **III.** ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in [0,1]$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $0<c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{-xt}-{x}^{3}t{e}^{-xt}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{|x-t|}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "333a10d9-6acd-51bc-a542-e5834510336a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466309} {:answer/id #uuid "0896e53d-ce4d-51df-a6c6-c42afcb5b091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0896e53d-ce4d-51df-a6c6-c42afcb5b091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466310} :answer-field/id #uuid "d140fccf-9864-5e7c-ab64-46dce6612c96" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466998} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf441900-eba8-530f-9a51-60c9dde68b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466311} {:answer/id #uuid "1b704fe9-df2a-5b5b-835b-671871eaa101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf441900-eba8-530f-9a51-60c9dde68b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466311} :answer-field/id #uuid "97b4a9dc-d50b-57ee-a809-22e3de81aa9c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466999} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc749d3b-d1da-5e3f-96d4-b8e2ecc22be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466313} {:answer/id #uuid "11dac17e-58fd-523c-9348-ca7babe92e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11dac17e-58fd-523c-9348-ca7babe92e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466314} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ebc44d1-abfd-536f-a11f-5b1f2580b68d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7963456-3a78-5141-94e0-577dece1c8b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-335249" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{3}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ So, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2}).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nSince $x\\in [0,1]$ and $t>0,$ we have $x+{t}^{2}>0,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t}{x+{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbf574b6-eadc-5800-be26-b717065fccf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466315} {:answer/id #uuid "47094ddd-c2bd-538f-ac32-cd8b1a036024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47094ddd-c2bd-538f-ac32-cd8b1a036024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466316} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7b8f2ac-6e53-518c-ac65-9b2a794b1a14" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467001} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b24a03-0840-5112-a6ae-85ba64feb1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466317} {:answer/id #uuid "19aece31-40c7-53b0-9e65-e7912b5a8f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19aece31-40c7-53b0-9e65-e7912b5a8f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466318} :answer-field/id #uuid "58af3294-3290-5a03-bc06-7906ebd614eb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467002} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b033fba9-aa64-512d-a47b-ee5a966fb49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466319} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e28aaa-7313-5516-adc5-06bd1a4249cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b033fba9-aa64-512d-a47b-ee5a966fb49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466319} :answer-field/id #uuid "e089d006-f109-5586-8f39-6db728f8f9b6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8de3c48f-01de-56bf-9ee6-1a3bd0e95cc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-335251" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n- **I.** ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+tx)}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (-1,1)$\n- **II.** ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (1,2)$\n- **III.** ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+tx)}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (-1,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-1,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ (since $1+tx>0$). Computing the  partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(1+tx)(1+{x}^{2})}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (1,2)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,2)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (1,2).$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{3}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21f7e4c1-6c8c-516f-99d4-3016aca7887a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466321} {:answer/id #uuid "a65ba9ef-0cc8-52d0-9799-3481656ed4a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a65ba9ef-0cc8-52d0-9799-3481656ed4a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466322} :answer-field/id #uuid "591c30be-8419-5dc6-80a9-275d90e3124a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9372c8d-42b6-5923-978a-5855e094b76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466323} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b3441a-93b7-54ca-a1d1-eb627c0c175b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9372c8d-42b6-5923-978a-5855e094b76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466323} :answer-field/id #uuid "7308a380-c9c9-5971-af9f-58b1d0cea85c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467005} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d96d0868-95f2-538f-a7e5-d8a250db5ce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466325} {:answer/id #uuid "9df9f9c9-4956-531c-b65e-ed180dad9e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9df9f9c9-4956-531c-b65e-ed180dad9e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466326} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b3e4b44-d0ed-53af-8a31-aa805a851393" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b175da7-6b72-569e-916d-da964786e33a" :question/math-academy-id "q-335252" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n- **I.** ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- **II.** ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$\n- **III.** ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{-{t}^{2}x}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-2tx{e}^{-{t}^{2}x},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f193bbe0-8e7c-5b6f-a8dd-105f7c96e087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466327} {:answer/id #uuid "047ad879-475c-51be-8100-93116e6fb609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "047ad879-475c-51be-8100-93116e6fb609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466328} :answer-field/id #uuid "c351f140-003f-5de1-a059-b4c452861f54" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467007} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac05226-c0db-52a3-ae14-60638c0dc180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466329} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe0749a-3fbf-53af-8a74-6c63016e53d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fe0749a-3fbf-53af-8a74-6c63016e53d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466330} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec4aa8ac-f5ec-5952-835d-341387814949" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467008} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dde5e19-36cd-5710-ac44-3b7ccc338aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466331} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4ce050-9839-5301-bc39-df7b6379da2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae4ce050-9839-5301-bc39-df7b6379da2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466332} :answer-field/id #uuid "52c91a3d-646c-5c9e-9c53-efcea5dea94b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0283e52-8fee-5c3a-a13d-c279ee722034" :question/math-academy-id "q-335253" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n- **I.** ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- **II.** ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xt)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- **III.** ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+t{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>-1$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ (since ${x}^{2}+{t}^{2}>0$ on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-\\frac{t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xt).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ so that $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xt)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+t{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>-1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{(1+t{x}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "958e50e4-711b-576c-ab91-0087c75945cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466333} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8fed5f-0e8b-5ec1-8918-492f6fc7c6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "958e50e4-711b-576c-ab91-0087c75945cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466333} :answer-field/id #uuid "5372760e-066f-55e0-b7b9-2ff11d55e2c0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467010} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "832c18d4-aaf4-5e10-9aeb-143c9e0d4b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466335} {:answer/id #uuid "51ef8b21-0c2d-5d7e-bac0-f3e5033cf5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51ef8b21-0c2d-5d7e-bac0-f3e5033cf5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466336} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aeac474-d1d6-5a95-a8c4-6a2ce80af73e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467011} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "800c1d5b-5d46-5618-b8ef-1b9451c13e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466337} {:answer/id #uuid "4250fbe7-e751-5a5c-88fa-cbb83498f5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4250fbe7-e751-5a5c-88fa-cbb83498f5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466338} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7db462d-3fc8-5643-b099-4624769f01bf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "757f1645-7b5c-5866-81f6-7ae1eefa124d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335254" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n- **I.** ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- **II.** ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- **III.** ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{1+{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{3}{(x+t+1)^{4}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a22ab5cf-13df-5925-8c56-24a2e18fabaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466339} {:answer/id #uuid "ff310a56-8a6f-5ed7-bf98-61e53df7dc8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a22ab5cf-13df-5925-8c56-24a2e18fabaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466339} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6103b29-3311-5514-bfba-8a71af2fd5aa" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467013} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ebac1c-34c4-5c6d-bb6c-785ac2249a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466341} {:answer/id #uuid "945abe1f-1a4c-598a-b831-113ac0528de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "945abe1f-1a4c-598a-b831-113ac0528de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466342} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbf27f1b-1445-5c81-aae4-f7f07eb27092" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467014} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9bbcda9-a787-548e-a44b-7fe7cb161bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466343} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb91fc8-2c32-5a9f-956a-db0acb298dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdb91fc8-2c32-5a9f-956a-db0acb298dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466344} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cbdaacc-92a5-5c61-9124-f11f072369ac" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7fa63bd-d15a-5bf7-b7e2-ddd1185810da" :question/math-academy-id "q-335255" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n- **I.** ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- **II.** ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- **III.** ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)={e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6827f68d-e970-5136-85fe-aa329ae42a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466345} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c0feb9-16ea-5599-8312-12db1aa4fc65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6827f68d-e970-5136-85fe-aa329ae42a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466345} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb993537-0261-5fda-b21a-d4228e3aa76b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467016} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c5f0cad-e6d7-5b07-9611-38cf67db5238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466347} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c1cfea-5b7f-572e-b8ac-986c5c727df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c5f0cad-e6d7-5b07-9611-38cf67db5238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466347} :answer-field/id #uuid "40ef3dc0-08f7-582a-805b-94e9383de941" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467017} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da544d1-339b-52fe-93ad-b7cc9037f9ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466349} {:answer/id #uuid "52870258-d972-5635-9243-e534e273e365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52870258-d972-5635-9243-e534e273e365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466350} :answer-field/id #uuid "00f9d316-8393-5444-ad7d-3bfe9ded25fc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e65967cd-c336-53ba-a105-1c4f34ff015c" :question/math-academy-id "q-335824" :question/problem "![](images/96/96df34b3f844ee5abace182769011a3b4b2e413100fbca4cf25e829ca67948cc.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6)$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=2,$ $y=4$ and $y=6.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (4,6).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y-4),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=2,4$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=2,4,6.$\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2).$ This does not correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4,6.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,6)$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(y-2)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (4,6),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3957a9c2-7880-520a-ade2-12150916fb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466351} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc83860-f67b-5819-93de-844ddfa746d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cc83860-f67b-5819-93de-844ddfa746d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466352} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdd77ac5-51c1-5cc5-80e6-bf64f824a81c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467019} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7971b98-4412-5cc0-966e-124b4ff95cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466353} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3b873e-9d8e-5426-95b8-9bb145cec8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7971b98-4412-5cc0-966e-124b4ff95cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466353} :answer-field/id #uuid "6206ecd4-d346-5c00-a75c-f1bfc159bcf0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467020} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a592ea9b-4320-545f-a7dc-64fa58ef1c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466355} {:answer/id #uuid "9282e269-a2f9-55a6-b6e5-cdfdeef3f9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9282e269-a2f9-55a6-b6e5-cdfdeef3f9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466356} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5bf6c60-1d15-5fd3-aa6e-0b7ca8f8930f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "870fa3ac-a172-57d1-b18b-7584f7771a44" :question/math-academy-id "q-337223" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n- **I.** $y=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+({y}^{′})^{2})$\n- **II.** $y{y}^{′}={x}^{3}y+1$\n- **III.** $y-x({y}^{′})^{2}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′}),\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+({y}^{′})^{2}).\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x({y}^{′})^{2}-{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a02ccdc-2375-5a29-92c3-175669696a30" :answer